### TRAP TABLE — Roll d10

All traps require a **DC 13 Passive Perception** to notice before triggering unless otherwise stated.

---

**1 — The Pressure Plate Field**

> *Three flat stones in the corridor floor are slightly discolored — older, smoother than the surrounding rock.*

- **Trigger:** Stepping on any plate
- **Effect:** A 10ft section of ceiling drops sharp stalagmite fragments. All creatures in a 10ft square make a DC 13 Dexterity save. Failure: 2d6 piercing. Success: half.
- **Spot DC:** 13 Perception. A DC 11 Investigation after noticing reveals all three plate locations.
- **Disarm:** DC 14 Thieves' Tools, or place weight on all three simultaneously (requires coordinating three characters — can hold boulders or packs in place)
- **After Trigger:** The plates are spent. The room is now safe but noticeably louder — any Horseman within 40ft investigates the sound (moves toward this room this round).

---

**2 — The Crystal Resonance Snare**

> *A fist-sized crystal sits in a small alcove in the wall. It pulses faintly blue.*

- **Trigger:** Moving within 5ft of the crystal, or making any sound louder than a whisper within 10ft
- **Effect:** The crystal emits a high-pitched shriek. All creatures within 20ft take 1d6 thunder damage and must succeed a DC 12 Constitution save or be Deafened for 1 minute. Additionally, every Horseman on the floor is alerted to this room's approximate location and moves toward it for 1d4 rounds.
- **Spot DC:** 11 Perception (the crystal is not hidden)
- **Disarm:** Wrapping it in cloth or leather (any padding) before approaching silences it. DC 12 Stealth to approach without triggering.
- **After Trigger:** The crystal goes dark and is inert.

---

**3 — The Collapse Ceiling (Weight-Sensitive)**

> *The floor here is covered in old rubble. The ceiling above is cracked. Crude wooden supports have been placed — they look old.*

- **Trigger:** More than 200 lbs of weight in the room at once (effectively: 2+ medium creatures)
- **Effect:** 1d4 sections of ceiling fall. Each creature in the room: DC 14 Dexterity save. Failure: 3d6 bludgeoning and Restrained under rubble (STR DC 15 to free themselves or be freed by ally as an action). Success: half damage, not Restrained.
- **Spot DC:** 14 Perception (the supports are visibly rotted — not obvious it's a trap vs. just dangerous)
- **Disarm:** Cannot be disarmed. Crossing one at a time (one person at a time, moving fast) avoids triggering. DC 12 Acrobatics to cross without pausing.
- **After Trigger:** The room is now partially collapsed and counts as Difficult Terrain.

---

**4 — The Memory Thief Mirror**

> *An intact oval mirror stands against one wall, ornately framed. Your reflection looks back at you — a half-second delayed.*

- **Trigger:** A spellcaster looks directly at their reflection while within 10ft
- **Effect:** DC 14 Wisdom save. On failure: one prepared spell slot (lowest available level) is temporarily "stolen" — the caster cannot use that slot for 1 hour but they feel no different and may not realize immediately. On a natural 1: they lose their highest available slot instead.
- **Spot DC:** DC 15 Arcana to notice the half-second reflection delay before it activates. DC 10 Perception notices the mirror exists.
- **Disarm:** Covering the mirror with cloth before looking. Any character who does not look directly at their own reflection (looks away, looks at someone else's reflection, closes eyes) is immune.
- **After Trigger:** The mirror cracks. Stolen spell slot returns in 1 hour.

---

**5 — The Adhesive Floor**

> *The stone floor in this section has a faintly yellowish tint and a slight sheen.*

- **Trigger:** Stepping onto the adhesive section
- **Effect:** Movement speed halved while in the area. DC 13 Strength save each round or become Restrained (stuck in place). Restrained creatures can use their action to attempt DC 15 Athletics to break free, or an ally can pull them (DC 14 Athletics as an action, both move at half speed).
- **Spot DC:** DC 12 Perception. DC 10 if carrying a light source (the sheen is more obvious in good light).
- **Disarm:** Any 2 gallons of liquid poured on the floor dissolves the adhesive. Water, oil, or alchemicals all work. The dissolved adhesive becomes Difficult Terrain (slippery) for 10 minutes.
- **Special:** Lumis is immune — its weight and the texture of its pads mean it passes through cleanly. Horsemen pass through but their mounts are slowed.

---

**6 — The Razor Wire Tripwire**

> *At shin height, almost invisible in the dim light, a near-transparent line runs across the corridor.*

- **Trigger:** Crossing the corridor without spotting it
- **Effect:** The triggering creature takes 1d4 slashing damage and falls Prone. If Prone in this corridor: all movement until standing costs double. Additionally, a small bell sounds — any Horseman within 50ft investigates.
- **Spot DC:** DC 15 Perception (it is specifically designed to be hard to see). DC 11 if using a light source held low.
- **Disarm:** Any bladed weapon or sharp item cuts it as a free action once spotted. Can also be stepped over (DC 12 Acrobatics).
- **After Trigger:** Wire is severed. Bell cannot be un-rung.

---

**7 — The Poison Dart Wall**

> *Several small holes in the south wall, evenly spaced. Easy to miss. Easy to regret missing.*

- **Trigger:** A pressure plate 10ft before the holes (DC 14 to spot before stepping)
- **Effect:** 1d4 darts fire at the triggering creature and all creatures within 5ft of them. Each dart: +5 to hit, 1d4 piercing. Any creature hit must succeed DC 14 Constitution save or be Poisoned for 1 hour.
- **Spot DC:** DC 14 for the plate. DC 12 for the holes (once you know to look for them).
- **Disarm:** DC 13 Thieves' Tools on the plate mechanism, or stuff the holes with cloth before crossing.
- **Darts:** 2d4 darts remain embedded in the wall after firing. Can be extracted and used as improvised thrown weapons (1d4 piercing, range 20/60, no poison remaining).

---

**8 — The Heartbeat Sensor Door**

> *A sealed stone door with no visible handle or lock — only a hand-carved image of a ribcage on its surface.*

- **Trigger:** This door only opens when all living creatures in the room hold perfectly still for 1 full minute (6 rounds of taking no actions, no movement, no speaking)
- **Effect (if failed):** A sharp vibration passes through the floor. All creatures: DC 11 Constitution save or take 1d4 thunder damage. The door remains sealed.
- **Bypass:** The door cannot be forced open (it's 2ft thick stone). Thieves' Tools cannot crack it. Knock spell opens it instantly.
- **On Success:** The door grinds open. It will close again in 10 minutes. Whatever is on the other side is worth the wait — the DM places a better-quality item (treat as rolling twice on the Item Table and taking the higher result) behind it.
- **Special:** If a Horseman enters the room while characters are holding still, the characters must still hold still to keep the effect going. Horsemen ignore still characters who are not attacking.

---

**9 — The Cave-In Trigger (Auditory)**

> *The ceiling here shows stress fractures. Several loose rocks are visible in the cracks above.*

- **Trigger:** Any loud noise (combat, shouting, running on stone) — DC 11 Dexterity save for each creature in the room
- **Effect:** Falling rocks. Failure: 2d8 bludgeoning. Success: half. The room is now partially collapsed (Difficult Terrain throughout).
- **Spot DC:** DC 10 Perception (the cracks are visible). DC 12 to predict it would be noise-triggered specifically.
- **Disarm:** Cannot be disarmed — the ceiling is genuinely unstable. Moving slowly and quietly through the room avoids triggering. DC 10 Stealth if moving normally, DC 7 if taking half speed.
- **Special:** If a Horseman gallops through this room, the cave-in triggers automatically.

---

**10 — The False Floor**

> *The stone here sounds slightly hollow if tapped (DC 12 Perception when stepping, DC 10 if specifically tapping with a weapon).*

- **Trigger:** Stepping on the false section with more than 50 lbs of weight
- **Effect:** 15ft drop into a lower chamber. DC 13 Dexterity save. Failure: 2d6 falling damage, land Prone in lower chamber. Success: catch the edge (hanging, require STR DC 12 to pull up or take the fall voluntarily for 1d6).
- **The Lower Chamber:** Contains 1 item (roll Item Table) and 1 junk (roll Junk Table). There is a climbable route back up (DC 11 Athletics, 1 minute). The lower chamber counts as a new room — roll for contents when first reached through the false floor.
- **After Trigger:** The hole remains. Other creatures can now see it and choose to descend or avoid it.

